<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Split</h1><p class="st-meta">1403 CE – 1413 CE · capital Split</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived Dalmatian duchy granted to the Bosnian magnate Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić, uniting Split and the central Dalmatian islands under one lord.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the struggle for the Hungarian crown between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund of Luxembourg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a>, the powerful Bosnian noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrvoje-vukcic-hrvatinic">Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić</a> backed Ladislaus. In 1403 Ladislaus rewarded him with the title of Duke of Split, together with the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brac">Brač</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hvar">Hvar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korcula">Korčula</a>, carving a duchy out of royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrvoje-vukcic-hrvatinic">Hrvoje</a> ruled from Split while remaining Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, so the duchy linked the inland Bosnian lands with the Adriatic trade of the city. Split kept its communal statutes and council. Hrvoje's court produced the richly illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrvojes-missal">Glagolitic Missal of Hrvoje</a>, made in Split around 1404, one of the finest Croatian medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrvoje-vukcic-hrvatinic">Hrvoje</a> later reconciled with Sigismund, but fell from favour and was declared a traitor in 1413; Split threw off his rule and returned to the Hungarian crown. Hrvoje died in 1416, and in 1420 Split passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which held it for nearly four centuries.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1403 CE – 1413 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.62%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1403 CE – 1413 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Split</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Croatian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hrvoje Vukčić's Split</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1408&amp;focus=duchy-of-split" aria-label="Duchy of Split on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5076 635 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Split lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5396 891L5437 871M5396 891L5446 783"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5437 871h0M5446 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5396 891h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5396 891h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Split. <a href="/atlas?year=1408&amp;focus=duchy-of-split">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
